WEBVTT

1
00:00:11.867 --> 00:00:17.456
Two days later the rainstorm ended.

2
00:00:17.457 --> 00:00:22.620
The wind stopped howling, and a brilliant blue sky stretched over the moor.

3
00:00:22.621 --> 00:00:29.101
The moor itself looked soft blue instead of gloomy purple-black or awful, dreary gray.

4
00:00:29.102 --> 00:00:33.036
When Mary awoke she sat up in bed and called to Martha.

5
00:00:33.037 --> 00:00:35.763
"Look at the moor!"

6
00:00:35.764 --> 00:00:41.686
"Yes," said Martha with a cheerful grin. "The storm's over for a bit.

7
00:00:41.687 --> 00:00:46.349
That happens this time of year. Spring is on its way."

8
00:00:46.350 --> 00:00:50.720
"I thought it always rained and looked dark in England," said Mary.

9
00:00:50.721 --> 00:01:00.099
"Oh, no. Just you wait until you see all the flowers blooming on the moor and butterflies fluttering and bees humming.

10
00:01:00.100 --> 00:01:04.722
You'll want to go out on the moor all day as Dickon does."

11
00:01:04.723 --> 00:01:09.955
Mary smiled. "I like Dickon, and I've never even met him."

12
00:01:09.956 --> 00:01:17.355
"All the animals like Dickon. I wonder," said Martha, "what he would think of you."

13
00:01:17.356 --> 00:01:23.121
"He wouldn't like me," Mary said stiffly. "No one does."

14
00:01:23.122 --> 00:01:27.210
Martha looked serious. "How do you like yourself?"

15
00:01:27.211 --> 00:01:31.042
Mary hesitated for a moment. "Not at all, really.

16
00:01:31.043 --> 00:01:33.770
But I never thought of that before."

17
00:01:33.771 --> 00:01:41.120
Martha grinned. "Mother asked me that once when I was in a bad temper and speaking ill of everyone.

18
00:01:41.121 --> 00:01:46.817
She said, 'There you stand saying you don't like this one and you don't like that one.

19
00:01:46.818 --> 00:01:52.656
How do you like yourself?' It made me laugh and brought me to my senses."

20
00:01:52.657 --> 00:01:57.011
Martha was in high spirits that morning because it was her day off.

21
00:01:57.012 --> 00:02:01.773
She gave Mary her breakfast, and then she was going home to the cottage.

22
00:02:01.774 --> 00:02:05.614
She wouldn't be back until the next morning.

23
00:02:05.615 --> 00:02:09.687
After Martha left, Mary felt lonelier than ever.

24
00:02:09.688 --> 00:02:15.840
She went outside, and the first thing she did was run around the fountain ten times.

25
00:02:15.841 --> 00:02:20.760
Then she ran into the kitchen gardens and found Ben Weatherstaff at work.

26
00:02:20.761 --> 00:02:24.682
The change in the weather seemed to have done him good.

27
00:02:24.683 --> 00:02:28.943
"Springtime's coming," he said cheerfully. "Can you smell it?"

28
00:02:28.944 --> 00:02:35.280
Mary sniffed the air. "I smell something nice and fresh and damp."

29
00:02:35.281 --> 00:02:39.449
"That's the good, rich earth," he said, digging away.

30
00:02:39.450 --> 00:02:43.740
"In the flower gardens, things will be stirring down there in the dark.

31
00:02:43.741 --> 00:02:48.898
The sun's warming them. You'll see bits of green sticking up soon."

32
00:02:48.899 --> 00:02:51.015
"What will they be?"

33
00:02:51.016 --> 00:02:55.748
"Crocuses and snowdrops and daffodils. Haven't you ever seen them?"

34
00:02:55.749 --> 00:03:02.856
"No," said Mary. "In India everything is hot and wet and green after the rains.

35
00:03:02.857 --> 00:03:06.094
Things there seem to grow up overnight."

36
00:03:06.095 --> 00:03:11.308
"These won't grow up in a night," said Ben. "You'll have to wait for them.

37
00:03:11.309 --> 00:03:15.867
They'll poke up a bit higher here and push out a bit more there.

38
00:03:15.868 --> 00:03:21.152
Then they'll uncurl a leaf this day and another one that day. You watch."

39
00:03:21.153 --> 00:03:28.557
"I'm going to." Mary heard the soft rustling of wings, and there was the robin looking right at her.

40
00:03:28.558 --> 00:03:31.986
"Do you think he remembers me?"

41
00:03:31.987 --> 00:03:36.606
"Remember you?" said Ben. "Of course he does."

42
00:03:36.607 --> 00:03:39.804
"Are things stirring in the garden where he lives?"

43
00:03:39.805 --> 00:03:43.016
"What garden?" Ben said gruffly.

44
00:03:43.017 --> 00:03:48.756
"The one where the old roses are." Mary couldn't help asking more questions.

45
00:03:48.757 --> 00:03:53.250
"Are all the flowers dead? Are there ever any roses?"

46
00:03:53.251 --> 00:03:56.182
"Ask the robin. He's the only one who knows.

47
00:03:56.183 --> 00:03:58.982
No one else has seen it for ten years."

48
00:03:58.983 --> 00:04:03.912
Ten years was a long time. She was ten years old herself.

49
00:04:03.913 --> 00:04:07.722
Mary slowly walked away, lost in thought.

50
00:04:07.723 --> 00:04:14.679
She had begun to like the garden, just as she had begun to like Dickon and Martha and their mother too.

51
00:04:14.680 --> 00:04:18.948
That seemed a lot of people to like when you weren't used to liking people.

52
00:04:18.949 --> 00:04:22.094
She also counted the robin as a person.

53
00:04:22.095 --> 00:04:28.315
She spotted him again as she was strolling along her favorite path outside the kitchen gardens.

54
00:04:28.316 --> 00:04:32.020
He was pecking in the earth and pretending that he hadn't followed her.

55
00:04:32.021 --> 00:04:34.593
But Mary knew he had.

56
00:04:34.594 --> 00:04:38.006
"You do remember me!" she cried.

57
00:04:38.007 --> 00:04:42.598
She chirped and talked and coaxed, while he hopped and twittered.

58
00:04:42.599 --> 00:04:46.719
The robin allowed her to move closer and closer to him.

59
00:04:46.720 --> 00:04:50.891
She bent down and tried to make robin sounds.

60
00:04:50.892 --> 00:04:53.863
The robin hopped under some shrubs.

61
00:04:53.864 --> 00:04:59.549
Then Mary saw him stop to look for a worm near a pile of freshly turned-up dirt.

62
00:04:59.550 --> 00:05:02.720
An animal must have been digging in the garden.

63
00:05:02.721 --> 00:05:08.725
As she watched the robin, she spotted something half-buried in the little pile of soil.

64
00:05:08.726 --> 00:05:13.016
It looked like a ring of brass or rusty iron.

65
00:05:13.029 --> 00:05:18.135
When the robin flew up into a nearby tree, Mary picked up the ring.

66
00:05:18.136 --> 00:05:22.184
But it wasn't a ring. It was an old key.

67
00:05:22.185 --> 00:05:26.004
Mary stared in amazement at the key in her hand.

68
00:05:26.005 --> 00:05:30.677
"Perhaps it has been buried for ten years," she whispered.

69
00:05:30.678 --> 00:05:38.238
"Perhaps this is the key to the garden."
